# Optional local config for start_bridge.sh -- most people don't need this
# file at all. bridge.py auto-discovers a supported device over BLE if you
# don't configure one, which is fine as long as exactly one is in range.
# Copy this file to config.sh only if you want to:
#   - combine sources, e.g. a ring with a watch fallback, or
#   - skip the ~10s discovery scan on every start (faster reconnects), or
#   - pin a specific device because more than one is in range
#
#   cp config.sh.example config.sh
#
# config.sh is gitignored on purpose: your BLE address identifies your
# specific physical device, so it doesn't belong in a repo others might
# fork, even though it isn't a secret in the password sense.
#
# Sources, highest priority first, separated by spaces -- see
# `python3 bridge.py --help` for every option. Find your device's exact
# value (it prints one per device in range) with:
#   .venv/bin/python3 bridge.py --list-devices
# SOURCES="viatom:REPLACE_ME hds"   # ring first, watch (HDS app) fallback

# Older single-device setting, still honored: the same as auto:ADDRESS.
# ADDRESS="REPLACE_ME"

# Uncomment to use a non-default WebSocket port (8766 here is just an
# example of a *different* value -- the actual default is 8765). If you
# change it, also update WS_URL at the top of overlay.html's <script>.
# WS_PORT="8766"

# OBS on another computer -- a cloud gaming PC, or a separate streaming PC.
# The bridge has to stay here, near your Bluetooth device, so this opens an
# SSH tunnel that makes that computer's localhost:WS_PORT lead back to this
# one's. Load overlay.html there as a local-file Browser Source exactly as
# you would here; nothing in it changes. See "OBS on another computer" in
# the README for what that computer needs (an SSH server and your key).
# Any ssh destination works; a Host alias from ~/.ssh/config keeps the user,
# key and address in one place:
# TUNNEL_HOST="user@100.64.0.10"
# TUNNEL_HOST="streaming-pc"
